[Tell us about a book, movie, or video you have recently enjoyed.] Just yesterday, I finished the last chapter of a book by Lloyd Geering called Such is Life. In this book, Geering, comma, who is a professor at New Zealand, scholar, and master in the presbyterian church of New Zealand, tells us about the evolution of the universe, from the Big Bang on through its increasing complexity into the realm of quantum mechanics. The first two chapters set forth his ideas about the evolving universe, and the last chapter, chapter twelve, speaks of the Axial Age, in which he sees we are now existing. Throughout the book, Gerring poses an imaginative interview with the author or authors of the biblical book called Ecclesiastes. He points out ways in which, what Ecclesiastes was talking about, reaches out to the modern day. And he's associates Ecclesiastes' author with some of the deep thinkers of our own time. I can't say I really enjoyed the book. I learned a lot from it, but those first two chapters, and the twelfth chapter, stand out as being the most valuable to me.
