[Please explain how you would play your favorite board game or card game.] As a child I didn't really have a favorite board game or card game. (Uh) My mother was very strict, German. And games were simply not allowed. If you had time to play games, you had too much time and you needed to have some work to be doing instead, was her motto. And one of the things I wished that we could've spent more time doing (if we did rarely) if we did do it, it was rare and is to put together a puzzle. I don't know where the puzzles came from, but they're quite old, quite worn, which means they're probably hand down from some relatives to us. And we would turn the box over, and dump the puzzle out, and then sort the pieces so they were upright. Look at the picture on the box and try to figure out what was showing and how many pieces would be in each part of the picture showing on the box. Like if it was a flower, how many puzzle pieces would it take to make up that flower? And then we'd start by pulling out the pieces that had the straight edges, because these were the pieces that formed the frame around the picture. And once this frame was in place, the rest of the pieces could easily pull together and put into place. And of course, the big highlight was finding the edges that had two square edges in a right angle because that meant you had a corner edge. And there were four of those to anchor the puzzle in every puzzle box. Hopefully, (h-) it hadn't been lost. And once that was done, you could look over the rest of the pieces and see if you could put together any part that was in the inside of the puzzle, perhaps a jacket or a hair or some article of something in the puzzle that you could put together on your own, and then build out from there.
