[Please explain the steps to plant a flower.] We have this lovely little marigold that we got at the nursery. And it needs to go into the ground (to) to stay alive. It doesn't do too well in this little pot all on its own. It needs a surrounding ground with fertilizer and water and sunlight. So, first we're going to prepare a spot for it by digging a hole and making sure it's deep enough for the plant to go into. Maybe a little bit recessed in about an inch or two so some water can gather at the top and sink on down. It would help if we could mix some fertilizer in but not too much because the plant's already growing and we don't want it to grow huge, we want it to be able to bloom. So we need some sort of a balanced fertilizer. And if you look on the back of the fertilizer box, it will tell you the proportions in there to nitrogen etcetera and what it is best used for. So, then you have to wet the pot with the plant in it so that it's nice and soaking wet so that the roots will be able to slide out of the container. If it's root bound, you'll have to unroot bound (it) it by tearing off some of the roots. And once you have it slid out of the container, you can put it in the ground, pat the dirt around it, and add some water so that it'll have the ground around it soaked as well as the ground that it's actually in, the dirt that it's actually potted in. And then stand back and watch it grow. Hopefully some sunlight will show up too and that would also help things along.
